Hardware Description Languages (HDLs) are used to describe the structure and behavior of digital circuits. Common HDLs include VHDL and Verilog. HDLs allow designers to model and simulate digital circuits before implementing them in hardware. HDLs also support synthesis, which is the process of converting an HDL description into a physical implementation. HDLs are essential tools for modern digital system design. Understanding the syntax and semantics of HDLs is crucial for designing and verifying digital circuits. Correct use of HDLs is essential for successful system development. Proper selection of HDL depends on the project requirements.

Example:
```verilog
module adder(input a, input b, output sum, output carry);
  assign sum = a ^ b;
  assign carry = a & b;
endmodule
```
